<!--
  The scheduled task Leash runs under, as the installer registers it.

  Leash needs administrator rights to stop a service. A task with the highest
  available rights starts the program at logon and shows no prompt, which a Run
  registry key cannot do, and a manifest asking for administrator rights would
  prompt at every launch.

  The two placeholders stand for the installed program, which is
  %ProgramFiles%\Leash\leash.exe unless another folder was chosen during the
  install. The installer writes its own copy with the real paths, and
  scripts/task-test.ps1 fills these in, so the task can be tried without
  installing anything.

  There is no XML declaration on purpose. Task Scheduler refuses a UTF-8 byte
  order mark outright, and it refuses a declaration that does not match the
  bytes, so a plain file with no declaration is the one form it accepts from
  this repository as well as from the installer, which writes its copy as
  UTF-16.

  ExecutionTimeLimit is PT0S, meaning no limit. Left out, Task Scheduler stops
  the program after 72 hours. MultipleInstancesPolicy is IgnoreNew for the same
  reason the app holds a single-instance lock: two engines watching the same
  services would fight.

  The settings that are not listed here keep the Task Scheduler defaults, which
  is what we want for idleness and wake behaviour. The battery settings are
  listed because their defaults would stop the task on a laptop.
-->
<Task version="1.4" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>Runs Leash at logon with the rights it needs to stop services.</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>HighestAvailable</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>__LEASH_EXE__</Command>
      <WorkingDirectory>__LEASH_DIR__</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
